course: dictionary
lesson: 14563
title: Body words
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Some words are about the body, and some are about the mind. Physiological is a body word. It means to do with how a living body works, such as breathing, heart rate, digestion and growing.
explain: «Her heart raced and her hands shook before the test. This was a physiological response to fear.» The racing heart and shaking hands happen in the body. To check, swap in the word body: the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Compare this with «She kept worrying about the test all night.» Worrying happens in the mind, so the word for that is psychological. Physiological is about the body, psychological is about the mind, and both can happen at the same time.
fact: Physiological means to do with how the body of a living thing works.
fact: Physiology is the noun: the study of how living bodies work.
fact: «Physiological changes» happen to your body as you grow up, such as in puberty.
fact: «Physiological needs» are things the body must have to live, such as food, water, sleep and warmth.
fact: Psychological means to do with the mind, and it is the partner word to physiological.
exercise: Which word means to do with how the body works? | Psychological | Imaginary | *Physiological | Emotional
exercise: «Food, water and sleep are physiological needs.» What does physiological mean here? | Written down | Having to do with the mind | *Having to do with how the body works | Expensive
exercise: Which is a physiological change as a child grows? | Feeling proud of a painting | Choosing a favourite colour | Remembering a holiday | *Growing taller
test: «Sweating and a fast pulse are physiological responses to fear.» What does physiological mean in this sentence? | Of the mind | Of school | Of the weather | *Of the body
test: Which word is about the mind, not the body? | Digestion | Physiological | Breathing | *Psychological
exam: Which of these is a physiological need? | Feeling loved | Fun friends | A favourite song | *Water, which the body needs to live
